1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,360
This is MATH 239, video 7a. The topic is formal power series: their algebra, inverses, and composition. We use the supplemental notes on formal power series, pages 1 to 4.

2
00:00:15,810 --> 00:00:19,394
After this video, you can do the six things in this list.

3
00:00:19,844 --> 00:00:25,967
Video 7b covers tutorial and midterm problems, square roots, and Taylor series.

4
00:00:26,417 --> 00:00:36,763
A formal power series is a sequence a sub 0, a sub 1, a sub 2, and so on, of complex numbers. We write it with powers of x.

5
00:00:37,213 --> 00:00:41,928
Draw it as a row of cells. Cell n holds the coefficient a sub n.

6
00:00:42,378 --> 00:00:54,047
The power x to the n only marks the position of a sub n. We never put a number in place of x. We only add series, multiply series, and read coefficients.

7
00:00:54,497 --> 00:00:59,041
In counting, a sub n is often the number of objects of weight n.

8
00:00:59,491 --> 00:01:07,342
The geometric series, 1 plus x plus x squared and so on, is the sequence 1, 1, 1, and so on.

9
00:01:07,792 --> 00:01:21,040
In calculus, x equal to 2 gives 1 plus 2 plus 4 plus 8, and so on. This sum has no value. As a formal power series there is no problem. It is only a list of numbers.

10
00:01:21,490 --> 00:01:31,047
A polynomial is also a formal power series. 1 plus 2 x plus x squared is the sequence 1, 2, 1, then zeros.

11
00:01:31,497 --> 00:01:34,719
Its coefficients are 0 after some position.

12
00:01:35,169 --> 00:01:43,382
The coefficients can be any complex numbers. When we solve equations with series, fractions and negative numbers appear.

13
00:01:43,832 --> 00:01:49,293
This row is a series whose square is 1 plus x. We meet it in video 7b.

14
00:01:49,743 --> 00:02:03,439
Two formal power series are equal if and only if all their coefficients are equal. So one equation between two series is a list of equations between numbers, one for each power of x.

15
00:02:03,889 --> 00:02:13,724
For example, expand 1 plus x, times 1 minus x, as you expand polynomials. You get 1, plus 0 x, minus x squared.

16
00:02:14,174 --> 00:02:21,641
The second series is 1 minus x squared. Its coefficients are 1, 0, minus 1, then zeros.

17
00:02:22,091 --> 00:02:28,640
Compare the two rows column by column. Every column agrees, so the two series are equal.

18
00:02:29,090 --> 00:02:37,389
The notation, the coefficient of x to the k in A of x, means the number a sub k. It is one number, not a series.

19
00:02:37,839 --> 00:02:43,108
Here the coefficient of x squared in 1 minus x squared is minus 1.

20
00:02:43,558 --> 00:02:52,411
The constant term has a second name: A of 0. A of 0 is the coefficient of x to the 0, which is a sub 0.

21
00:02:52,861 --> 00:03:02,909
A of 0 is only a name for the constant term. It does not mean that we put 0 into a function. The theorems in this video use this name.

22
00:03:03,359 --> 00:03:08,053
The sum of two series adds the coefficients of the same power of x.

23
00:03:08,503 --> 00:03:13,495
Here A of x is the geometric series: 1, 1, 1, and so on.

24
00:03:13,945 --> 00:03:18,467
B of x is 1 minus x: 1, minus 1, then zeros.

25
00:03:18,917 --> 00:03:24,699
Add column by column. Column x to the 0 gives 1 plus 1, which is 2.

26
00:03:25,149 --> 00:03:28,989
Column x to the 1 gives 1 minus 1, which is 0.

27
00:03:29,439 --> 00:03:37,353
Every other column gives 1 plus 0, which is 1. So the sum is 2, 0, 1, 1, 1, and so on.

28
00:03:37,803 --> 00:03:45,953
The product multiplies every term of A by every term of B, as for polynomials. The grid shows all the products.

29
00:03:46,403 --> 00:03:49,133
Row i is the term a sub i x to the i.

30
00:03:49,583 --> 00:03:53,338
Column j is the term b sub j x to the j.

31
00:03:53,788 --> 00:04:04,583
The cell i, j is a sub i x to the i, times b sub j x to the j. That is a sub i b sub j, x to the i plus j.

32
00:04:05,033 --> 00:04:10,494
For example, cell 1, 2 holds a 1 b 2. It is a term of x cubed.

33
00:04:10,944 --> 00:04:25,643
To find the coefficient of x cubed, collect the cells with i plus j equal to 3. They lie on one purple diagonal: a 0 b 3, a 1 b 2, a 2 b 1, and a 3 b 0.

34
00:04:26,093 --> 00:04:38,893
In general, the coefficient of x to the k in A times B is the sum over i from 0 to k of a sub i times b sub k minus i. This is the product formula.

35
00:04:39,343 --> 00:04:50,585
It has exactly k plus 1 terms, so each coefficient is a finite number. So the product of two formal power series is always a formal power series.

36
00:04:51,035 --> 00:04:57,179
Here is an example. Find the square of the geometric series. Pause the video and try it.

37
00:04:57,629 --> 00:05:06,995
Both factors are the geometric series, so every coefficient is 1. So every cell of the grid is 1 times 1, which is 1.

38
00:05:07,445 --> 00:05:20,138
The coefficient of x cubed is the sum on the diagonal i plus j equal to 3: a 0 b 3, plus a 1 b 2, plus a 2 b 1, plus a 3 b 0.

39
00:05:20,588 --> 00:05:27,009
The diagonal has four cells, each equal to 1. So the coefficient of x cubed is 4.

40
00:05:27,459 --> 00:05:36,270
In general, the diagonal for x to the k has k plus 1 cells. So the coefficient of x to the k is k plus 1.

41
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:41,264
So the square is 1 plus 2 x plus 3 x squared, and so on.

42
00:05:41,714 --> 00:05:54,493
Now we solve an equation. Example 2.1 asks for a series B of x with 1 minus 2 x, times B of x, equal to 1 plus x. Pause the video and try it.

43
00:05:54,943 --> 00:06:01,876
Write B of x with unknown coefficients b sub 0, b sub 1, b sub 2, and so on.

44
00:06:02,326 --> 00:06:07,211
Write the right side as 1 plus x plus 0 x squared, and so on.

45
00:06:07,661 --> 00:06:14,467
The known factor, 1 minus 2 x, has only two nonzero coefficients: 1 and minus 2.

46
00:06:14,917 --> 00:06:25,562
So by the product formula, the coefficient of x to the k in the product is 1 times b sub k, plus minus 2 times b sub k minus 1.

47
00:06:26,012 --> 00:06:31,943
Now compare the columns. Column x to the 0 gives b sub 0 equal to 1.

48
00:06:32,393 --> 00:06:41,929
Column x to the 1 gives b sub 1 minus 2 b sub 0 equal to 1. So b sub 1 is 1 plus 2, which is 3.

49
00:06:42,379 --> 00:06:49,888
For k at least 2, the right side is 0. So b sub k equals 2 times b sub k minus 1.

50
00:06:50,338 --> 00:06:57,485
So each new coefficient is 2 times the one before: 6, 12, 24, 48, and so on.

51
00:06:57,935 --> 00:07:13,380
So B of x equals 1 plus 3 x plus 6 x squared, and so on. The coefficient of x to the k is 3 times 2 to the k minus 1. For k equal to 3, that is 3 times 4, which is 12.

52
00:07:13,830 --> 00:07:21,873
Each equation forced exactly one value. So we found a solution, and we also proved that it is the only solution.

53
00:07:22,323 --> 00:07:31,816
Example 2.2 changes the known factor to x minus 2 x squared. Pause the video and try to solve it with the same method.

54
00:07:32,266 --> 00:07:34,805
The known factor has constant term 0.

55
00:07:35,255 --> 00:07:46,817
By the product formula with k equal to 0, the constant term of the product is 0 times b sub 0. That is 0, for every choice of b sub 0.

56
00:07:47,267 --> 00:07:50,083
But the constant term of 1 plus x is 1.

57
00:07:50,533 --> 00:07:55,739
Zero is not equal to 1. So no series B of x solves the equation.

58
00:07:56,189 --> 00:08:02,269
The notes say: a zero constant term of the known factor is the only problem that can occur.

59
00:08:02,719 --> 00:08:22,303
Theorem 2.1 states the general result. Let A of x and C of x be formal power series. If A of 0 is not 0, then exactly one series B of x satisfies A of x times B of x equals C of x. The method of Example 2.1 proves it.

60
00:08:22,753 --> 00:08:36,107
By the definition of equality and the product formula, the equation means: for every k, the sum over i from 0 to k of a sub i b sub k minus i equals c sub k.

61
00:08:36,557 --> 00:08:43,213
Take the term with i equal to 0 out of the sum. It is a sub 0 times b sub k.

62
00:08:43,663 --> 00:08:53,882
A sub 0 is not 0, so we can divide by it. So b sub k equals c sub k minus the rest of the sum, all over a sub 0.

63
00:08:54,332 --> 00:09:02,609
For k equal to 0, the rest of the sum is empty. So b sub 0 equals c sub 0 over a sub 0.

64
00:09:03,059 --> 00:09:11,465
For k at least 1, the rest of the sum uses only b sub 0 up to b sub k minus 1. These are already known.

65
00:09:11,915 --> 00:09:17,739
For example, take b sub 3. The rest of the sum has a sub 1 times b sub 2.

66
00:09:18,189 --> 00:09:20,535
It has a sub 2 times b sub 1.

67
00:09:20,985 --> 00:09:23,652
And it has a sub 3 times b sub 0.

68
00:09:24,102 --> 00:09:32,529
Subtract these terms from c sub 3, and divide by a sub 0. Here we need a sub 0 not equal to 0.

69
00:09:32,979 --> 00:09:44,797
So by induction on k, each b sub k has exactly one value. These values satisfy every equation. So the series B of x exists, and it is unique.

70
00:09:45,247 --> 00:09:57,557
In calculus you saw the formula: 1 over 1 minus x equals 1 plus x plus x squared, and so on. For formal power series, the left side has no meaning yet.

71
00:09:58,007 --> 00:10:01,761
We only have a sum and a product. There is no division.

72
00:10:02,211 --> 00:10:10,787
So the notes define an inverse. The inverse of A of x is a series B of x with A of x times B of x equal to 1.

73
00:10:11,237 --> 00:10:16,571
Here 1 is the series 1 plus 0 x plus 0 x squared, and so on.

74
00:10:17,021 --> 00:10:21,906
We write B of x as 1 over A of x, or A of x to the minus 1.

75
00:10:22,356 --> 00:10:31,423
Check the geometric series G of x. 1 minus x, times G of x, is G of x minus x times G of x.

76
00:10:31,873 --> 00:10:39,766
Multiplication by x moves every coefficient one place to the right. The minus sign makes each one minus 1.

77
00:10:40,216 --> 00:10:43,416
Add the two rows. The constant term is 1.

78
00:10:43,866 --> 00:10:49,199
Every other column is 1 minus 1, which is 0. So the product is 1.

79
00:10:49,649 --> 00:11:00,188
So the geometric series is the inverse of 1 minus x. The formula from calculus is now a true statement about formal power series.

80
00:11:00,638 --> 00:11:10,174
Theorem 3.1 says which series have an inverse. The inverse of A of x exists if and only if A of 0 is not 0.

81
00:11:10,624 --> 00:11:15,360
And if the inverse exists, it is unique. The proof has two cases.

82
00:11:15,810 --> 00:11:18,562
Case 1: A of 0 is not 0.

83
00:11:19,012 --> 00:11:22,511
Use Theorem 2.1 with C of x equal to 1.

84
00:11:22,961 --> 00:11:31,707
It gives exactly one series B of x with A of x times B of x equal to 1. So the inverse exists, and it is unique.

85
00:11:32,157 --> 00:11:35,208
Its constant term is 1 over a sub 0.

86
00:11:35,658 --> 00:11:38,047
Case 2: A of 0 is 0.

87
00:11:38,497 --> 00:11:46,241
Then for every series B of x, the constant term of the product is 0 times b sub 0, which is 0.

88
00:11:46,691 --> 00:11:52,921
But the constant term of 1 is 1. So no B of x works, and A of x has no inverse.

89
00:11:53,371 --> 00:12:07,515
Because the inverse is unique, the notation 1 over A of x names exactly one series. So if two methods both give an inverse, they give the same coefficients. We use this later in this video.

90
00:12:07,965 --> 00:12:17,053
Now compute an inverse. Find the first coefficients of 1 over 1 minus x plus 2 x squared. Pause the video and try it.

91
00:12:17,503 --> 00:12:22,367
The constant term is 1, so the inverse exists, by Theorem 3.1.

92
00:12:22,817 --> 00:12:32,182
The rows of the grid are the terms of A of x: 1, minus x, and 2 x squared. The row 0 x cubed is all zeros.

93
00:12:32,632 --> 00:12:41,123
The columns are the terms of the unknown inverse: b sub 0, b sub 1 x, and so on. Each cell is a product.

94
00:12:41,573 --> 00:12:52,069
The product must be 1. So the diagonal for x to the 0 must add to 1. It has one cell, b sub 0. So b sub 0 equals 1.

95
00:12:52,519 --> 00:13:03,121
The diagonal for x to the 1 must add to 0. It is b sub 1 minus b sub 0. So b sub 1 equals b sub 0, which is 1.

96
00:13:03,571 --> 00:13:18,483
The diagonal for x squared is b sub 2 minus b sub 1 plus 2 b sub 0. It must be 0. So b sub 2 is b sub 1 minus 2 b sub 0: 1 minus 2, which is minus 1.

97
00:13:18,933 --> 00:13:33,675
The diagonal for x cubed is b sub 3 minus b sub 2 plus 2 b sub 1, plus 0. So b sub 3 is b sub 2 minus 2 b sub 1: minus 1 minus 2, which is minus 3.

98
00:13:34,125 --> 00:13:39,885
So the inverse is 1 plus x minus x squared minus 3 x cubed, and so on.

99
00:13:40,335 --> 00:13:55,161
The general rule: b sub n equals minus the sum of a sub i b sub n minus i, for i from 1 to n, divided by a sub 0. Later in this video we get the same numbers in a second way.

100
00:13:55,611 --> 00:14:04,187
Here is practice problem P2-1 from Assignment 2. Which of these four series has an inverse? Pause the video and try it.

101
00:14:04,637 --> 00:14:10,803
By Theorem 3.1, look only at the constant terms, in column x to the 0.

102
00:14:11,253 --> 00:14:17,375
A of x is x plus x squared. Its constant term is 0, so it has no inverse.

103
00:14:17,825 --> 00:14:23,415
B of x is 1 plus x. Its constant term is 1, so the inverse exists.

104
00:14:23,865 --> 00:14:29,731
C of x has constant term 3 to the 0, which is 1. So the inverse exists.

105
00:14:30,181 --> 00:14:40,656
D of x is C of x minus 1. So its constant term is 1 minus 1, which is 0, and it has no inverse. These are the posted answers.

106
00:14:41,106 --> 00:14:50,109
We can also find the inverses. The inverse of 1 plus x is 1 minus x plus x squared minus x cubed, and so on.

107
00:14:50,559 --> 00:15:03,380
Multiply back to check. For n at least 1, the coefficient of x to the n is minus 1 to the n, plus minus 1 to the n minus 1. That is 0. So the product is 1.

108
00:15:03,830 --> 00:15:08,673
The inverse of C of x is the polynomial 1 minus 3 x squared.

109
00:15:09,123 --> 00:15:20,237
Check: for m at least 1, the coefficient of x to the 2 m in the product is 3 to the m, minus 3 times 3 to the m minus 1. That is 0.

110
00:15:20,687 --> 00:15:28,389
The odd powers of x have coefficient 0 in both factors, so they stay 0. So the product is 1.

111
00:15:28,839 --> 00:15:34,961
The composition A of B of x replaces each x in A of x with the series B of x.

112
00:15:35,411 --> 00:15:44,201
So A of B of x is a sub 0, plus a sub 1 times B of x, plus a sub 2 times B of x squared, and so on.

113
00:15:44,651 --> 00:15:52,181
This is an infinite sum of series. It is a formal power series only if each coefficient is a finite number.

114
00:15:52,631 --> 00:16:00,311
Here is an example from the notes. Take A of x as the geometric series, and B of x as 1 plus x.

115
00:16:00,761 --> 00:16:10,724
Then A of B of x is 1, plus 1 plus x, plus the square of 1 plus x, and so on. The table shows these powers of 1 plus x.

116
00:16:11,174 --> 00:16:16,977
Look at column x to the 0. Every power of 1 plus x has constant term 1.

117
00:16:17,427 --> 00:16:29,160
So the constant term of the composition is 1 plus 1 plus 1, and so on, with no end. This is not a number. So this composition is not a formal power series.

118
00:16:29,610 --> 00:16:39,637
The cause is the constant term of B of x. It is 1, not 0. The next theorem shows that a zero constant term fixes the problem.

119
00:16:40,087 --> 00:16:52,439
Theorem 3.2. Let A of x and B of x be formal power series. If B of 0 is 0, then A of B of x is a well defined formal power series.

120
00:16:52,889 --> 00:16:58,734
The table shows the powers of B of x, for B of x equal to x plus x squared.

121
00:16:59,184 --> 00:17:11,344
Here is the proof. B of 0 is 0, so every term of B of x contains x. So B of x equals x times C of x, for a formal power series C of x.

122
00:17:11,794 --> 00:17:15,570
For x plus x squared, C of x is 1 plus x.

123
00:17:16,020 --> 00:17:25,001
Put this into the definition. The term a sub k, B of x to the k, becomes a sub k, x to the k, C of x to the k.

124
00:17:25,451 --> 00:17:32,513
So the k-th power starts at x to the k or later. The green staircase marks these zeros.

125
00:17:32,963 --> 00:17:47,043
Now take the coefficient of x to the n. Every term with k greater than n starts after x to the n, so it adds 0. In the table, rows 4 and 5 add 0 to column x cubed.

126
00:17:47,493 --> 00:17:55,493
Only the terms with k from 0 to n are left. So column x cubed uses only rows 0 to 3.

127
00:17:55,943 --> 00:18:02,428
So each coefficient is a finite sum of numbers, and A of B of x is a formal power series.

128
00:18:02,878 --> 00:18:09,790
The first line of the proof in the notes has a misprint: a sub 2 B of x should be a sub 1 B of x.

129
00:18:10,240 --> 00:18:22,507
Here is an example. A of x is the geometric series, and B of x is x plus x squared. Find the first coefficients of A of B of x. Pause the video and try it.

130
00:18:22,957 --> 00:18:28,738
B of 0 is 0, so the composition is well defined, by Theorem 3.2.

131
00:18:29,188 --> 00:18:34,415
All a sub k are 1. So A of B of x is the sum of the rows of the table.

132
00:18:34,865 --> 00:18:37,489
For x cubed, add column x cubed.

133
00:18:37,939 --> 00:18:40,115
Rows 0 and 1 give 0.

134
00:18:40,565 --> 00:18:44,106
Row 2, the square of x plus x squared, gives 2.

135
00:18:44,556 --> 00:18:46,007
Row 3 gives 1.

136
00:18:46,457 --> 00:18:50,702
Rows 4 and 5 start after x cubed, so they give 0.

137
00:18:51,152 --> 00:18:57,083
So the coefficient of x cubed is 0 plus 0 plus 2 plus 1, which is 3.

138
00:18:57,533 --> 00:19:03,378
Do the same for every column. The column sums are 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8.

139
00:19:03,828 --> 00:19:09,865
So A of B of x is 1 plus x plus 2 x squared plus 3 x cubed, and so on.

140
00:19:10,315 --> 00:19:20,043
The method: for the coefficient of x to the n, keep the rows k up to n. Multiply the entry in column n by a sub k, and add.

141
00:19:20,493 --> 00:19:29,987
The notes give a second way to find an inverse. Write 1 minus x plus 2 x squared as 1 minus the series x minus 2 x squared.

142
00:19:30,437 --> 00:19:36,815
Then 1 over it is the geometric series with x replaced by x minus 2 x squared.

143
00:19:37,265 --> 00:19:47,569
This is a composition with B of x equal to x minus 2 x squared. B of 0 is 0, so by Theorem 3.2 it is well defined.

144
00:19:48,019 --> 00:19:54,590
The table shows the powers of B of x, up to x cubed. Row k starts at x to the k.

145
00:19:55,040 --> 00:19:58,752
Add the columns. Column x to the 0 gives 1.

146
00:19:59,202 --> 00:20:01,314
Column x to the 1 gives 1.

147
00:20:01,764 --> 00:20:06,265
Column x squared gives minus 2 plus 1, which is minus 1.

148
00:20:06,715 --> 00:20:11,302
Column x cubed gives minus 4 plus 1, which is minus 3.

149
00:20:11,752 --> 00:20:17,512
So the inverse is 1 plus x minus x squared minus 3 x cubed, and so on.

150
00:20:17,962 --> 00:20:22,741
These are the same coefficients as before, when we used Theorem 2.1.

151
00:20:23,191 --> 00:20:27,735
They must agree, because the inverse is unique, by Theorem 3.1.

152
00:20:28,185 --> 00:20:40,366
Assignment problem A2-1. Prove: if A of x times B of x is the zero series, then A of x is 0 or B of x is 0. Pause the video and try it.

153
00:20:40,816 --> 00:20:45,659
Use a proof by contradiction. Suppose that both series are not zero.

154
00:20:46,109 --> 00:20:55,154
Let i be the smallest index with a sub i not 0. In the grid, i is 2, so the rows a 0 and a 1 are all 0.

155
00:20:55,604 --> 00:21:04,948
Let j be the smallest index with b sub j not 0. In the grid, j is 1, so the column b 0 is all 0.

156
00:21:05,398 --> 00:21:20,566
Look at the coefficient of x to the i plus j. By the product formula, it is the sum on the purple diagonal: a sub k times b sub i plus j minus k, for k from 0 to i plus j.

157
00:21:21,016 --> 00:21:26,541
For k less than i, a sub k is 0. These cells are in the zero rows.

158
00:21:26,991 --> 00:21:36,655
For k greater than i, the index i plus j minus k is less than j. So that b is 0. This cell is in the zero column.

159
00:21:37,105 --> 00:21:49,095
Only the cell with k equal to i is left. So the coefficient is a sub i times b sub j. Both factors are not 0, so the coefficient is not 0.

160
00:21:49,545 --> 00:21:57,417
This contradicts the fact that A of x times B of x is 0. So A of x is 0, or B of x is 0.

161
00:21:57,867 --> 00:22:08,384
In words: the lowest terms of two nonzero series multiply to the lowest term of the product. Video 7b uses this fact for square roots.

162
00:22:08,834 --> 00:22:16,941
To recap. A formal power series is a list of coefficients. Two series are equal when every coefficient is equal.

163
00:22:17,391 --> 00:22:23,855
The coefficient of x to the k is one entry of the list, and A of 0 is the constant term.

164
00:22:24,305 --> 00:22:29,190
The product formula adds the cells on one diagonal of the product grid.

165
00:22:29,640 --> 00:22:38,963
Theorem 2.1 solves A of x times B of x equals C of x, one column at a time, when A of 0 is not 0.

166
00:22:39,413 --> 00:22:47,391
Theorem 3.1: an inverse exists if and only if the constant term is not 0, and it is unique.

167
00:22:47,841 --> 00:22:59,084
Theorem 3.2: a composition A of B of x is a formal power series when B of 0 is 0. With the geometric series, it gives inverses.

168
00:22:59,534 --> 00:23:06,702
By A2-1, a product of two nonzero series is not zero, because the lowest terms multiply.
